    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116830,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 317,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most prominent and constant physical feature is the small size (height, weight, and head size). Also common are a lack of subcutaneous fat tissue, and a rash, most commonly on the cheeks and nose, usually first appearing during infancy after sun exposure {28846287}. A high-pitched voice and distinctive facial features (long, narrow face; prominent nose and ears; small lower jaw) can be observed. Most affected individuals have normal intellectual ability. Men tend to be infertile (azoospermia and oligospermia), whereas women may be fertile but often have early menopause. Individuals with Bloom syndrome have mild immune system abnormalities (often low IgM and IgA levels) leading to recurrent infections of the upper respiratory tract, ears, and lungs during infancy and are at greater-than-normal risk of chronic obstructive lung disease and diabetes mellitus resembling the adult-onset type. In addition, they are 150&ndash;300&nbsp;times as likely to develop cancers as are normal individuals (66% solid tumours, 33% leukaemia/lymphoma). Colorectal cancer is the most common tumour in the Bloom syndrome registry (29&nbsp;cases), followed by skin, breast, and oropharyngeal cancer (24&nbsp;cases each) {17407155}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T15:42:41"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116831,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 317,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Bloom syndrome, first described in New York City in 1954 by dermatologist David Bloom, is extremely rare, with about 283&nbsp;cases reported to the Bloom Syndrome Registry {17407155}. Although the overall prevalence is unknown, in the Ashkenazi Jewish population it is estimated to be approximately 1&nbsp;case&nbsp;per 48&nbsp;000&nbsp;births. A founder mutation known as <em>BLM</em>(Ash) is present in approximately 1&nbsp;in 100&nbsp;people of Ashkenazi Jewish background. There are also founder mutations in the Slavic and Hispanic populations.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T15:42:41"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116832,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 317,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Caused by mutations in the gene <em>BLM</em>, on 15q26.1, which encodes a member of the RECQ family of DNA helicases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T15:42:41"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116833,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 317,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Loss of BLM function results in the accumulation of errors during DNA replication and increased chromosomal rearrangements and breakages, leading to genetic instability and predisposing individuals to cancer {33736941}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T15:42:41"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
